n\nCopy\n\n@misc{kastreva2025tokenisationboundedalphabetshard, title={Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard}, author={Violeta Kastreva and Philip Whittington and Dennis Komm and Tiago Pimentel}, year={2025}, eprint={2511.15709}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CL}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15709}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2511-15709.html)\nciteturn2487search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # \"Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard.\" ... (2025)\n\n# \"Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nVioleta Kastreva et al. (2025)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nTrier\n\n  * Dagstuhl\n\n  * > Home\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2511.15709\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2026-02-01\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2511-15709\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2511-15709\n\nVioleta Kastreva, Philip WhittingtonImage: 0009-0005-0910-6826, Dennis Komm, Tiago Pimentel:\nTokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard. CoRR abs/2511.15709 (2025)\n\nImage\n\nlast updated on 2026-02-01 13:37 CET by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2511.16599)\nciteturn2487search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; ## #1 Time dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{2}] [REL] ... Publish: 2025-11-20 17:55:21 UTC\n# 2511.16599\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Time dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{2}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: Lukas Billera, Hedwig Nora Nordlinder, Ben Murrell\n\nThis brief note clarifies that, in Generator Matching (which subsumes a large family of flow matching and diffusion models over continuous, manifold, and discrete spaces), both the Bregman divergence loss and the linear parameterization of the generator can depend on both the current state $X_t$ and the time $t$, and we show that the expectation over time in the loss can be taken with respect to a broad class of time distributions. We also show this for Edit Flows, which falls outside of Generator Matching. That the loss can depend on $t$ clarifies that time-dependent loss weighting schemes, often used in practice to stabilize training, are theoretically justified when the specific flow or diffusion scheme is a special case of Generator Matching (or Edit Flows). It also often simplifies the construction of $X_1$-predictor schemes, which are sometimes preferred for model-related reasons. We show examples that rely upon the dependence of linear parameterizations, and of the Bregman divergence loss, on $t$ and $X_t$.\n\nSubjects: Machine Learning , Machine Learning\n\nPublish: 2025-11-20 17:55:21 UTC\n\n#1 Time dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublished as a paper at the 2nd DeLTa Workshop, ICLR 2026 (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=COJIuG0eRN)\nciteturn2487search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; In the notation of Edit Flows (EF) (Havasi et al., 2025), we let ut(x, z|xt, zt) generate pt(x, z) on ... where a(t), b(t) > 0 are internally reweighting the loss and w(t) > 0 is externally reweighting the ... While time-dependent loss reweighting is often used in practice, it is sometimes only heuristically\n, we have:\n∇θExt,zt∼pt(x,z)[D(\n1\nh(t)\nP\nz ut(·, z|xt, zt), vθ\nt (·|xt))] = ∇θExt∼ptD(\n1\nh(t)ut(·|xt), vθ\nt (·|xt))\nfor any Bregman divergence D. The loss minimum is found at vθ∗\nt (·|xt) :=\n1\nh(t)ut(·|xt), implying\nthat vθ\nt (·|xt) can be used to parametrize edit rates via uθ\nt(·|xt) := h(t)vθ\nt (·|xt), whereby we recover\nthe marginal rates at the loss minimum:\nuθ∗\nt (·|xt) = h(t)vθ∗\nt (·|xt) = ut(·|xt).\n5\nDISCUSSION\nWhile time-dependent loss reweighting is often used in practice, it is sometimes only heuristically\nor empirically justified. Here we have clarified that, for a large class of generative models, it is also\ntheoretically justified. We further clarify that the linear parameterization of the generator, and the\nBregman divergence loss, can both depend on both the time and the state of the process.\n6\nACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThis project received support from the Swedish Research Council (2024-00390 and 2023-02516)\nand the Knut and Alice Wallenberg Foundation (2024.0039) to B.M.\n8--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#nlp #machinelearning #llms #algorithms #complexitytheory #ethzurich #research #firstpaper | Violeta Kastreva | 32 comments (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/violetakastreva_nlp-machinelearning-llms-activity-7403104888034881536-35qH)\nciteturn2487search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; 🎉 My first paper, “Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard”, is now online. ... 📄 Paper link in the comments 👇 #NLP #MachineLearning #LLMs #Algorithms #ComplexityTheory #ETHZurich #Research #FirstPaper\n\n# Violeta Kastreva’s Post\n\nVioleta Kastreva\n\n7mo\n\nI finally get to write this sentence: my first Research Paper is out! 🎉 My first paper, “Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard”, is now online. Tokenisers are a vital part of LLMs, but how hard is it to find an optimal one? 🤔 Prior work showed that with arbitrarily large alphabets, the problem is NP-hard. In this paper we ask what happens in much more realistic settings - like bytes, or strings such as a, aa, aaa, … We show that this is still hard, in a strong sense: for binary alphabets, not only is finding an optimal tokeniser NP-hard, but so is finding arbitrarily good approximations; and for unary alphabets, even finding an optimal direct tokeniser is NP-hard. So if you were hoping for a general algorithm that always finds the “best” tokeniser for your data, theory is not on your side - heuristics and approximations are here to stay. Huge thanks to my co-authors Philip Whittington, Dennis Komm and Tiago Pimentel at ETH Zürich for their collaboration and mentorship on this first paper, and to the ETH Student Summer Research Fellowship programme for the opportunity to work together. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nETH Library (https://www.research-collection.ethz.ch/bitstreams/efdd8ed0-8e68-49fc-bf62-3d84a10a6c55/download)\nciteturn2487search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Whether these results hold once alphabet sizes are bounded by a constant is thus left open. ... We now move to the analysis of tokenisation over bounded alphabets. ... We then prove that unary direct tokenisation is NP-complete (in §5).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n有界アルファベット上のトークン化は困難である〖JST機械翻訳〗 | 文献情報 | J-GLOBAL 科学技術総合リンクセンター (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/public/202502210840394191)\nciteturn2487search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Tokenisation over Bounded Alphabets is Hard ... 発行年： 2025年11月19日  プレプリントサーバーでの情報更新日： 2025年11月20日\n"
